WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.362 --> 00:00:04.646
Upstream prevention is designed to stop problems before they explode.

2
00:00:05.066 --> 00:00:09.222
But in America, emergency care drives astronomical medical bills.

3
00:00:09.642 --> 00:00:10.886
Take aging housing.

4
00:00:11.274 --> 00:00:15.814
Damp walls and mold quietly trigger expensive, preventable asthma attacks.

5
00:00:16.266 --> 00:00:19.910
But treating root causes is high-yield economic infrastructure.

6
00:00:20.394 --> 00:00:23.270
Every one dollar invested in lead hazard control

7
00:00:23.562 --> 00:00:28.006
produces up to $221 in measurable downstream benefits.

8
00:00:28.394 --> 00:00:32.710
Yet 90 percent of U.S. health care dollars go to unmanaged conditions.

9
00:00:33.034 --> 00:00:38.182
By waiting for emergencies, we outspend every wealthy nation, yet live shorter lives.

10
00:00:38.666 --> 00:00:42.214
But when public health is treated as infrastructure, the math shifts.

11
00:00:42.698 --> 00:00:45.606
Targeted interventions intercept the cost multiplier.

12
00:00:46.122 --> 00:00:48.646
The bill for an unmanaged crisis doesn't disappear, but

13
00:00:49.066 --> 00:00:53.350
preventing it upstream saves hundreds of thousands in downstream hospital costs.

14
00:00:53.866 --> 00:00:56.614
So public health isn't just a luxury cost on a ledger.

15
00:00:56.970 --> 00:01:00.454
It is the invisible economic shield protecting our entire future.

16
00:01:00.938 --> 00:01:03.238
Human in purpose. Systemic in method.

17
00:01:03.978 --> 00:01:04.806
Protective in outcome.
